odels can then utilize these features to distinguish individuals with Parkinson’s disease from healthy controls by identifying characteristic movement patterns [9]. Studies conducted in real-world environments have demonstrated the reliability of wearable monitoring systems in detecting gait disturbances and motor fluctuations during everyday activities, thereby offering improved ecological validity compared to laboratorybased assessments. The increasing availability of wearable sensor data has further stimulated the development of machine learning approaches for PD detection. These algorithms are capable of uncovering hidden patterns in high-dimensional biomedical datasets and constructing predictive diagnostic models. Parkinsonian motion signals exhibit complex temporal characteristics such as tremor oscillations, gait irregularities, and bradykinesia, which are difficult to capture using traditional statistical techniques. Machine learning methods, however, can learn discriminative representations directly from the data, enabling automated classification of motor symptoms. Early studies employed classical supervised learning algorithms such as Support Vector Machines (SVM), Random Forests (RF), and k-Nearest Neighbors (k-NN), successfully demonstrating the feasibility of objective PD detection based on gait features, tremor patterns, and accelerometer measurements. Subsequent research expanded these approaches to large-scale datasets collected in real-world environments. Wearable monitoring frameworks were developed to detect early symptoms of Parkinson’s disease by analyzing movement irregularities and balance disturbances using classical machine learning models. Ensemble learning strategies were also explored, combining multiple models to improve classification robustness and reduce prediction variance by leveraging complementary decision boundaries. These approaches demonstrated improved predictive performance, particularly when integrating multimodal datasets