e, allowing it to learn complex dependencies without relying on strict sequential processing. Transformer models have therefore attracted increasing interest in healthcare applications involving biomedical signal analysis. These architectures can effectively capture both short-range and long-range patterns within high-dimensional data, making them well suited for Parkinson’s disease detection. By assigning adaptive attention weights, Transformer-based models can emphasize relevant motion patterns while suppressing noise in wearable sensor signals. Hybrid CNN– Transformer architectures combine the strengths of both approaches by integrating localized feature extraction with global contextual modeling, thereby providing more comprehensive representations of complex motor patterns in wearable monitoring frameworks. In addition to predictive accuracy, interpretability is a critical requirement for clinical AI systems. Gradient-based attribution methods have emerged as promising techniques for explaining deep learning predictions. Integrated Gradients (IG) is an axiomatic interpretability approach that computes feature importance by integrating gradients along a path between a baseline input and the actual data instance. This method satisfies important theoretical properties such as sensitivity and implementation invariance, making it suitable for explaining deep neural network predictions. In the context of Parkinson’s disease analysis, IG can help identify clinically meaningful biomarkers such as tremor frequency and abnormal motion dynamics extracted from sensor or voice data. By highlighting influential signal segments, interpretability techniques improve transparency and foster trust in intelligent healthcare systems [16]. Alongside accuracy and interpretability, security and data integrity are essential considerations for modern digital healthcare systems. AI-driven diagnostic frameworks often rely on large volumes of sensitive patient data collected from wearable devices, electronic health records, and remote monitoring platforms. Protecting this information from unauthorized access, manipulation, or loss is crucial for ensuring reliable clinical decision support. However, centralized data storage architectures are inherently vulnerable to security breaches, single-point failures, and unauthorized data modifications. Such vulnerabilities may compromise diagnostic reliability and undermine trust in automated healthcare systems.
