1.1. Research gap
Recent progress in wearable monitoring technologies and artificial intelligence-based analysis has significantly advanced Parkinson’s disease (PD) detection. However, several critical limitations persist in the current literature. Many existing studies primarily prioritize classification accuracy by applying machine learning (ML) or deep learning (DL) models to sensor-based signals, demonstrating promising performance in identifying motor patterns associated with PD. Despite these improvements, the research focus remains largely predictive, while essential requirements for real-world deployment are often overlooked. In addition, handcrafted feature engineering is still widely used, which restricts the ability of models to generalize effectively across diverse populations and varying monitoring conditions [7,9]. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) are particularly effective in capturing localized temporal and spatial characteristics of motion signals; however, they often struggle to model long-range temporal dependencies in extended time-series sequences [8]. Consequently, more advanced architectures are required that can integrate robust local feature extraction with global contextual modeling in order to fully capture the complex temporal dynamics associated with Parkinson’s disease. Another important limitation in many PD detection systems is the lack of interpretability. Deep learning models are frequently treated as ‘‘black-box’’ systems, making it difficult for clinicians to understand how predictions are generated. This lack of transparency raises concerns regarding reliability and trust, ultimately limiting the clinical acceptability of such systems in sensitive healthcare environments where decision accountability is crucial. As a result, explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) methods have been increasingly emphasized to provide interpretable insights and enable validation of diagnostic outcomes [16]. However, the integration of XAI techniques within wearable-based PD detection models remains limited. Most existing frameworks generate predictive outputs without offering clear explanations regarding the specific signal segments or motion patterns that drive the model’s decisions. In addition to interpretability challenges,
data security and integrity issues have received comparatively less attention in wearable health monitoring systems. Wearable devices continuously generate large volumes of sensitive physiological and behavioral data that must be securely stored, processed, and transmitted. Many existing implementations rely on centralized architectures, which are vulnerable to unauthorized modification, data breaches, and limited auditability. Such vulnerabilities can compromise diagnostic reliability and raise concerns regarding patient privacy and system trustworthiness. Blockchain technology has recently been proposed as a potential solution for ensuring secure and tamper-proof data storage through decentralized ledger mechanisms. Nevertheless, the integration of blockchain with deep learning frameworks for Parkinson’s disease detection remains relatively limited [18,19]. Developing unified frameworks that simultaneously address predictive accuracy, interpretability, and data integrity therefore remains an open research challenge. This motivates the need for integrated systems that combine advanced deep learning architectures, explainable AI techniques, and blockchain-based mechanisms to enable secure, interpretable, and reliable diagnostic solutions. Despite significant advances in wearable sensing, deep learning, and digital healthcare technologies, several critical challenges continue to limit the effectiveness of intelligent Parkinson’s disease detection systems. First, conventional machine learning and CNN-based approaches primarily focus on local feature extraction and often struggle to capture long-range temporal dependencies inherent in wearable inertial sensor signals, leading to incomplete modeling of Parkinsonian movement dynamics. Second, many deep learning models operate as black-box systems, providing limited insight into the underlying decision-making process, which restricts clinical trust and adoption. The lack of transparent explanations makes it difficult for clinicians to validate whether model predictions are based on clinically meaningful movement patterns. Third, existing wearable healthcare frameworks typically rely on centralized data storage architectures that remain vulnerable to unauthorized modification, data tampering, and single-point failures, raising concerns regarding the integrity, traceability, and security of diagnostic records. Furthermore, most existing studies address predictive performance, interpretability, and data security independently rather than within a unified framework. These limitations highlight the need for an integrated solution capable of simultaneously modeling complex temporal dependencies, providing clinically interpretable predictions, and ensuring secure management of diagnostic outcomes. To address these challenges, this study proposes a BlockchainEnabled Hybrid CNN–Transformer framework with Integrated Gradients for Parkinson’s disease detection using smartwatch-based inertial sensor data. The CNN module is employed to learn discriminative local motion features, while the Transformer module captures long-range temporal relationships within movement signals through self-attention mechanisms. Integrated Gradients is incorporated to improve interpretability by identifying the signal regions that contribute most significantly to classification decisions. In addition, a lightweight blockchainbased integrity verification layer is introduced to provide immutable storage, traceability, and tamper-resistant management of diagnostic predictions. By jointly integrating predictive modeling, explainability, and security, the proposed framework aims to establish a reliable, interpretable, and trustworthy Parkinson’s disease detection system suitable for real-world healthcare applications.
