2. Related works
2.1. Wearable sensor-based Parkinson’s monitoring
Wearable sensing technologies have become an important component of modern Parkinson’s disease (PD) monitoring, enabling continuous, objective, and real-world measurement of motor activity. Parkinson’s disease is characterized by several motor impairments, including tremor, bradykinesia, gait abnormalities, and postural instability, which often fluctuate throughout the day. Conventional clinical assessment methods typically rely on standardized rating scales and intermittent observations during hospital visits, which may fail to capture subtle variations in symptom severity. Wearable devices address these limitations by providing continuous monitoring through sensors such as inertial measurement units (IMUs), accelerometers, and gyroscopes. These sensors capture high-resolution time-series data during patients’ daily activities, enabling the recording of limb movements, gait dynamics, and tremor oscillations. As a result, objective digital biomarkers can be derived, supporting earlier diagnosis, improved long-term tracking of disease progression, and the detection of subtle irregularities in natural environments outside clinical settings [22,23]. Several wearable platforms have been investigated in the literature, including wrist-worn smartwatches, body-mounted IMUs, and instrumented insoles. These systems facilitate the extraction of clinically relevant metrics such as tremor frequency, stride length, gait speed, and step symmetry. Inertial sensors are particularly valuable for quantitative gait analysis, providing detailed information about turning duration, stride variability, and step timing. Such parameters serve as reliable biomarkers for assessing disease severity and evaluating
treatment effectiveness [22]. Moreover, wearable systems enable the detection of complex motor events such as freezing of gait, dyskinesia, and tremor episodes through the analysis of accelerometer and gyroscope signals during real-world activities. This approach improves ecological validity by capturing patient behavior outside controlled laboratory environments, thereby supporting more accurate disease assessment. Recent studies have further extended the application of wearable sensing technologies toward early detection and continuous monitoring of Parkinson’s disease through advanced data analytics and machine learning techniques. Smartwatch-based systems, for example, have been used to analyze upper-limb tremor patterns and differentiate Parkinson’s disease from other movement disorders. Inertial measurements also provide valuable indicators of early motor impairment, including variations in gait speed, stance duration, and postural stability. Artificial intelligence techniques enhance the identification of complex motion signatures and enable predictive modeling of symptom progression. Magneto-inertial devices have demonstrated strong performance in detecting symptoms such as akinesia, tremor, and dyskinesia through continuous signal analysis [24]. Despite these promising developments, several challenges remain. Real-world motion signals obtained from wearable sensors are often affected by noise, variations in sensor placement, and differences in patient behavior, which complicate signal interpretation. Additionally, the data generated by wearable devices are typically high-dimensional and temporally complex, requiring advanced computational frameworks for effective analysis. Processing long time-series sequences and extracting meaningful representations from such data necessitate robust machine learning and deep learning models. Consequently, recent research has increasingly focused on integrating wearable sensing technologies with advanced computational approaches to improve detection accuracy and enable scalable remote monitoring solutions [25].
2.2. Machine learning approaches for Parkinson’s disease detection
Machine learning techniques have been widely explored for automated Parkinson’s disease (PD) detection and monitoring. In many early studies, sensor-based motion data were analyzed to identify hidden patterns within high-dimensional datasets derived from wearable devices. Wearable signals are particularly suitable for machine learning applications because they capture complex motor behaviors associated with PD. Initial research efforts focused on building classification models capable of differentiating between PD patients and healthy controls by analyzing gait dynamics, tremor characteristics, and acceleration patterns. Various supervised learning algorithms, including Support Vector Machines (SVM), Random Forests, Decision Trees, and k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), were applied to model nonlinear relationships in the data. These models typically relied on statistical descriptors and frequency-domain features extracted from motion signals to identify motor impairments associated with Parkinson’s disease [4, 9]. Subsequent investigations expanded these frameworks by utilizing wearable sensor data such as accelerometer and gyroscope measurements to detect early-stage symptoms of the disease. Researchers analyzed gait variability, step timing irregularities, and tremor oscillations to quantify disease severity and identify specific manifestations such as bradykinesia and freezing of gait. These symptoms often produce characteristic signatures within sensor signals that can be detected through supervised learning models when well-engineered features are extracted from the recordings [7,11]. In addition, ensemble learning techniques were introduced in several studies, where multiple models were combined to improve classification reliability. These ensemble strategies helped reduce prediction variance and enhanced the generalization capability of the models when applied to heterogeneous datasets [10].
