2.3. Deep learning-based Parkinson’s disease detection
Deep learning has advanced rapidly in recent years, significantly enhancing biomedical data analysis by enabling the extraction of hierarchical representations from high-dimensional datasets. These models are capable of identifying intricate patterns that are often difficult to detect using traditional machine learning approaches. As a result, deep learning has become highly beneficial for Parkinson’s disease (PD) detection across multiple data modalities, including wearable sensor signals, speech recordings, handwriting analysis, and gait dynamics. By learning multi-level features directly from raw data, deep learning models can effectively capture nonlinear relationships while reducing dependence on handcrafted feature engineering. In particular, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) have demonstrated strong performance in this domain due to their ability to detect local temporal and spatial patterns through convolutional filtering operations [12]. Several CNN-based frameworks have been explored for processing wearable sensor data, particularly signals obtained from accelerometers and gyroscopes. These models are capable of extracting discriminative features that correspond to clinically relevant motion patterns such as tremor oscillations, gait irregularities, and abnormal motor dynamics. Data obtained from smartwatch measurements have been widely used to train such models, often achieving higher classification accuracy compared to conventional machine learning techniques. The ability of CNN architectures to capture localized signal characteristics enables effective identification of subtle motor abnormalities associated with PD [8]. Furthermore, multi-scale network designs have been introduced to simultaneously analyze both frequency-domain and timedomain representations of sensor signals, allowing the detection of subtle physiological abnormalities with improved robustness [13]. To further enhance modeling capability, hybrid deep learning architectures have been investigated by integrating convolutional networks with recurrent structures such as Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks. In these hybrid frameworks, CNN layers perform local feature extraction, while LSTM layers capture sequential dependencies and temporal relationships across signal sequences. This combination enables the modeling of complex motion patterns associated with disease progression and motor fluctuations in PD patients [14]. Although these hybrid architectures improve temporal modeling capability, certain challenges remain, particularly in capturing long-range dependencies within extended time-series data and ensuring interpretability in clinical decision-making environments.
2.4. Explainable artificial intelligence in Parkinson’s disease diagnosis
The adoption of deep learning in medical diagnostic systems has increased significantly in recent years. However, as these models become more widely deployed, interpretability has emerged as a critical requirement for clinical acceptance. Although deep learning approaches often achieve remarkable predictive performance, their internal decision-making processes remain largely opaque. This lack of transparency presents a major challenge in healthcare environments, where diagnostic decisions must be reliable, interpretable, and clinically justifiable. Parkinson’s disease (PD) detection systems commonly analyze various forms of physiological data, including wearable sensor recordings, speech signals, and gait patterns, to identify disease-related abnormalities. Despite their effectiveness, clinicians require clear explanations of how these models arrive at specific predictions in order to trust and integrate them into medical workflows. Consequently, Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) has emerged as an important research direction aimed at improving model transparency and enhancing the trustworthiness of AI-driven diagnostic systems [20]. XAI techniques enable the interpretation of model predictions by identifying the contributions of individual input features. Several widely used approaches, including SHAP, LIME, and gradient-based explanation methods, have been applied to understand deep learning models.
Among these, Integrated Gradients (IG) has gained particular attention due to its strong theoretical foundation and practical effectiveness. IG works by integrating gradients along a path from a baseline input to the actual input, thereby quantifying the importance of each feature in influencing the model’s prediction. This approach satisfies key interpretability properties, including sensitivity and implementation invariance, making it particularly suitable for explaining deep neural networks in biomedical applications [17]. Recent studies have explored the use of interpretable frameworks for Parkinson’s disease detection, enabling the identification of clinically meaningful biomarkers within complex physiological signals. These approaches highlight important signal components and provide both visual and numerical explanations for model predictions. As a result, physiologically relevant patterns can be verified, while potential noise or spurious correlations are minimized during the interpretation process [16]. In particular, voice-based PD diagnosis has benefited from explainability methods, as they reveal informative speech characteristics associated with disease progression. Such insights not only enhance the reliability of the diagnostic system but also support clinical understanding of the underlying physiological mechanisms.
2.5. Blockchain-based security in healthcare data management
Digital healthcare systems are expanding rapidly, accompanied by a significant growth in wearable monitoring technologies. As a result, large volumes of sensitive health data are continuously generated, stored, transmitted, and analyzed. Parkinson’s disease (PD) monitoring systems, in particular, collect tremor signals, gait patterns, and other neurological indicators derived from wearable sensors. These data are highly sensitive, making privacy protection and secure handling essential. Preventing unauthorized manipulation is therefore critical. However, traditional centralized storage infrastructures remain vulnerable to several security challenges, including single-point failures, unauthorized access, and potential data tampering. Such vulnerabilities raise serious concerns regarding system reliability and the integrity of stored information. Since AI-driven diagnostic and predictive systems depend heavily on accurate and trustworthy data, the need for secure and tamper-resistant data management frameworks has become increasingly urgent. Blockchain technology has emerged as a promising solution to address these challenges. As a decentralized and transparent technology, blockchain provides immutable data storage through a distributed ledger maintained across multiple network nodes. In this architecture, transactions are organized into blocks that are cryptographically linked to one another, ensuring that any attempt to alter stored information would require consensus across the network. This mechanism eliminates reliance on a single central authority while significantly enhancing data integrity and traceability. In the healthcare domain, blockchain can facilitate secure data sharing among hospitals, clinicians, and researchers while maintaining strict access control policies. Consequently, the authenticity of medical data can be preserved and verified throughout its lifecycle [19]. The blockchain validation process ensures that all verified transactions become tamper-proof and permanently auditable, thereby strengthening trust in digital healthcare systems. Recent studies have explored the integration of blockchain with artificial intelligence to improve the reliability and transparency of medical data processing pipelines. In such frameworks, electronic health records (EHRs), remote monitoring data, and AI-generated diagnostic outputs can be securely stored and validated through blockchain-based mechanisms. Blockchain-enabled systems have therefore been proposed to ensure the integrity of datasets used for training and deploying AI models for disease prediction. Additionally, traceable verification of diagnostic outcomes can be achieved through cryptographic hashing, which securely records prediction results on the distributed ledger. This approach enables the creation of immutable clinical decision records,
