3. Proposed methodology
This study proposes a secure, explainable, and scalable deep learning framework for Parkinson’s Disease movement signal classification using smartwatch inertial sensor data integrated with blockchain-based integrity verification.
3.1. System overview
The proposed architecture combines temporal convolutional feature extraction, Transformer-based sequential modeling, frequency-domain feature fusion, explainable artificial intelligence (XAI), and decentralized blockchain storage. Wearable inertial sensors provide continuous and objective measurement of motor impairments in Parkinson’s disease, overcoming limitations of subjective clinical scales such as the Unified Parkinson’s Disease Rating Scale (UPDRS) [22]. Deep learning architectures, particularly Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), have demonstrated strong performance in learning hierarchical representations from biomedical time-series signals [12]. However, CNNs have limited capability in modeling long-range temporal dependencies. Transformer architectures address this limitation using self-attention mechanisms capable of capturing global temporal relationships [26]. Furthermore, explainability is essential in medical AI systems for clinical trust and interpretability. Integrated Gradients (IG), a gradientbased attribution method, provides reliable explanations by computing feature importance relative to a baseline [17]. To ensure data integrity
