Fig. 1 illustrates the overall architecture of the proposed CNN– Transformer framework for Parkinson’s disease (PD) detection integrated with blockchain-based result validation. The pipeline begins with wearable sensor data acquisition, where raw multi-dimensional time-series signals are collected and formatted for analysis. These signals undergo segmentation and preprocessing, including normalization and noise mitigation, to ensure consistent input representation. The processed data are then forwarded to a convolutional neural network (CNN) module, which performs hierarchical feature extraction to capture local temporal patterns and motion-specific characteristics. The extracted feature maps are subsequently passed to the Transformer encoder, where self-attention mechanisms model long-range temporal dependencies and global contextual relationships within the signal. The fused representations are then propagated to a fully connected classification layer that produces the final prediction output, categorized as PD or No PD. Finally, the prediction results are securely recorded on a blockchain ledger to ensure immutability, traceability, and integrity verification. This architecture enables robust feature learning, improved classification performance, and secure clinical decision support through decentralized validation.
3.2. Dataset description
