3.4. Unsupervised label generation using K-means
K-Means clustering was employed as an unsupervised pre-labeling mechanism to partition extracted signal embeddings into structurally coherent groups prior to supervised model training. This strategy reduces reliance on manual annotations while uncovering latent patterns in wearable inertial sensor data associated with Parkinsonian motor characteristics. After preprocessing and feature extraction, the highdimensional feature vectors were standardized to ensure balanced contribution across dimensions. The K-Means algorithm iteratively assigns each sample to the nearest centroid using a distance-based similarity measure and updates cluster centroids to minimize intra-cluster variance. The optimal number of clusters was determined using internal validation techniques such as the elbow method and silhouette coefficient analysis to ensure compact and well-separated group formations. The resulting cluster assignments were used as pseudo-labels and aligned with clinical categories through minimal supervision, enabling their integration into the CNN–Transformer training pipeline. This semi-supervised approach enhances scalability and robustness, particularly in medical datasets where labeled samples are limited. Although K-Means assumes convex cluster structures and equal variance distribution, conditions that may not fully capture nonlinear biomedical signal patterns, it provided efficient and stable structural grouping in this study. Incorporating unsupervised clustering strengthened feature organization, improved downstream classification readiness, and contributed to the model’s overall generalization performance in Parkinson’s disease detection. K-means clustering partitions signals into K clusters by minimizing intra-cluster variance [27]:
