3.6.2. Wavelet transform
Wavelet Transform (WT) is a powerful time–frequency analysis technique that enables multi-resolution decomposition of non-stationary signals, making it particularly suitable for biomedical time-series such as Parkinsonian tremor signals. Unlike the Fourier Transform, which provides only global frequency information, wavelet transform preserves both temporal and spectral characteristics by decomposing the signal into scaled and shifted versions of a mother wavelet. This property allows precise localization of transient events, abrupt motor fluctuations, and tremor-dominant frequency components within wearable sensor data. By analyzing high-frequency components with fine temporal resolution and low-frequency components with broader temporal windows, WT effectively captures multi-scale motion dynamics. In this study, wavelet-based feature extraction enhances discriminative representation by isolating clinically relevant oscillatory patterns while suppressing noise, thereby improving downstream classification performance within the proposed deep learning framework. Wavelet transform provides time–frequency localization [29]:
