3.7. Deep learning architecture
The proposed deep learning architecture integrates convolutional neural networks, a Transformer encoder, an attention pooling mechanism, and fully connected classification layers to achieve robust Parkinson’s disease detection. Initially, the CNN component performs hierarchical feature extraction from raw inertial sensor signals, capturing localized spatial–temporal patterns such as tremor oscillations and motion irregularities. The extracted feature maps are then fed into a Transformer encoder, which models long-range temporal dependencies through self-attention mechanisms, enabling the network to assign adaptive importance to different time segments of the signal. To further enhance discriminative representation, an attention pooling layer aggregates the most informative features into a compact embedding while suppressing redundant or low-importance activations. Finally, fully connected layers perform nonlinear feature transformation and output probabilistic predictions through a SoftMax activation function. This hybrid architecture effectively combines local pattern recognition with global context modeling, resulting in improved classification accuracy and generalization performance.
3.7.1. Convolutional neural network feature extraction
The Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) component is employed as the primary feature extractor to capture local temporal patterns from wearable inertial sensor signals. By applying one-dimensional convolutional filters across sliding windows of the time-series input, the CNN learns discriminative representations associated with short-term motor fluctuations, tremor oscillations, and micro-movement irregularities characteristic of Parkinson’s disease. The convolutional layers perform localized receptive field operations, enabling automatic extraction of low-level features such as signal amplitude variations and frequencyspecific oscillations, which are subsequently transformed into higherlevel abstract representations through hierarchical stacking. Activation
