3.10. Loss function and optimization
The proposed CNN–Transformer model was trained using the crossentropy loss function, which is widely adopted for multi-class and binary classification problems due to its effectiveness in quantifying the divergence between predicted probabilities and true class labels. Crossentropy penalizes incorrect predictions with higher confidence more severely, thereby encouraging the network to produce well-calibrated probability distributions. This formulation enhances class separability and accelerates convergence during training. For parameter optimization, the Adaptive Moment Estimation (Adam) optimizer was employed. Adam combines the advantages of momentum-based gradient descent and adaptive learning rate scaling by maintaining firstand second-order moment estimates of the gradients. This results in faster convergence, improved stability, and efficient handling of sparse gradients, making it particularly suitable for deep hybrid architectures such as the proposed CNN–Transformer framework.
