4.3. Decision threshold and error analysis
The proposed CNN–Transformer framework performs binary classification using the probability scores generated by the final Softmax layer. A decision threshold of 0.5 was adopted, whereby samples with predicted probabilities greater than or equal to 0.5 were classified as Parkinson’s disease (PD), while those below the threshold were classified as non-PD. This threshold represents a commonly adopted operating point that balances sensitivity and specificity in medical classification tasks. The resulting performance metrics demonstrated strong discriminative capability, achieving an accuracy of 97.29%, precision of 97.14%, recall of 97.29%, and F1-score of 97.12%. The high recall indicates that the model successfully identified the majority of Parkinson’s disease cases, thereby minimizing the occurrence of false negatives. A detailed analysis of classification errors revealed that false positives and false negatives were relatively limited compared with the total number of evaluated samples. False positive predictions may arise from movement patterns in healthy individuals that resemble Parkinsonian motor characteristics, whereas false negatives are more clinically significant because they correspond to Parkinson’s disease cases that remain undetected by the system. The low false negative rate observed in the proposed framework suggests that the model effectively captures disease-specific temporal and motion-related features, reducing the likelihood of missed diagnoses. This behavior can be attributed to the complementary strengths of CNN-based local feature extraction and Transformer-based long-range temporal dependency modeling.
