4.9. Explainable AI (XAI) results — integrated gradients
To enhance model transparency and facilitate clinical interpretability, Integrated Gradients (IG) was employed to identify the temporal regions of the inertial sensor signals that contributed most significantly to the model’s predictions. Integrated Gradients computes feature attributions by accumulating gradients along a path from a predefined baseline input to the actual signal. In this study, a zero-reference baseline signal was utilized during the attribution process, representing the absence of motion activity across all accelerometer and gyroscope channels. This baseline was selected because it provides a neutral reference point for evaluating the contribution of movement-related patterns within the sensor data. Fig. 11 presents the Integrated Gradients attribution map generated for representative Parkinson’s disease samples. The horizontal axis represents time (seconds), while the vertical axis denotes the normalized sensor signal amplitude. The color intensity corresponds to the magnitude of the attribution score, where warmer regions indicate stronger contributions to the final classification decision. High-attribution regions are concentrated within specific temporal segments characterized by oscillatory movement patterns and abnormal signal fluctuations. These regions received greater attention from the CNN–Transformer
