5. Discussion
The proposed Blockchain-Integrated CNN–Transformer framework demonstrates strong predictive capability for Parkinson’s disease detection using wearable inertial sensor data. The model achieved high accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and AUC, indicating robust class discrimination and balanced predictive behavior. The consistency between precision and recall suggests that the classifier is neither biased toward positive nor negative predictions, which is critical in medical diagnostics where both false negatives and false positives carry clinical consequences. Furthermore, the narrow confidence interval reflects low
statistical variance and stable generalization across evaluation samples. Collectively, these results confirm that the proposed architecture provides reliable and reproducible performance. The performance gains can be attributed to the hybrid architectural design combining convolutional layers with Transformer-based attention mechanisms. The convolutional component effectively extracts localized spatial–temporal patterns from inertial signals, such as tremor oscillations and abrupt motor fluctuations. In contrast, the Transformer module enhances long-range temporal dependency modeling by leveraging self-attention mechanisms that assign adaptive importance weights across the entire signal sequence. This global context modeling overcomes limitations commonly observed in traditional recurrent architectures, such as gradient decay and sequential processing constraints. The synergy between local feature extraction and global dependency learning significantly improves representation quality and classification robustness compared to standalone CNN, LSTM, or conventional machine learning models. Explainability analysis using Integrated Gradients further strengthens the validity of the proposed system. The attribution maps indicate that high-contribution regions correspond to physiologically meaningful oscillatory segments within the sensor signals. These segments align with known Parkinsonian motor characteristics, including tremor-dominant frequency patterns and irregular movement dynamics. The interpretability results suggest that the model’s decisions are driven by clinically relevant signal components rather than noise artifacts or spurious correlations. This level of transparency enhances practitioner trust and supports the integration of the framework into clinical decision-support environments. A distinguishing contribution of this work is the integration of a blockchain-based integrity layer. Unlike conventional AI-based diagnostic systems that focus solely on predictive performance, the proposed framework incorporates secure hashing and block validation to ensure immutability of prediction records. The measured average transaction latency was negligible, demonstrating that the security enhancement does not compromise inference efficiency. Successful integrity verification confirms that stored predictions remain tamperproof and auditable. This feature is particularly significant in healthcare contexts where data integrity, traceability, and regulatory compliance are essential. By embedding blockchain within the AI pipeline, the framework transitions from a predictive model to a secure, trustworthy medical AI system. From a practical standpoint, the framework is suitable for wearable healthcare deployment. The low computational overhead during inference supports real-time monitoring applications. The explainability module provides interpretable outputs for clinician review, while the blockchain layer ensures accountability and medico-legal traceability. The combination of high predictive accuracy, interpretability, and security establishes the proposed system as a comprehensive solution for intelligent Parkinson’s disease monitoring.
