7. Conclusion
This study presented a secure and interpretable deep learning framework for Parkinson’s disease detection using wearable inertial sensor data. The proposed Blockchain-Integrated CNN–Transformer architecture was designed to address three critical requirements in modern healthcare AI systems: high predictive accuracy, model interpretability, and data integrity assurance. By combining convolutional feature extraction with Transformer-based global attention mechanisms, the framework effectively captured both local motion dynamics and longrange temporal dependencies inherent in Parkinsonian motor patterns. The experimental results demonstrated superior classification performance, achieving high accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and AUC, thereby confirming strong discriminative capability and generalization stability. Beyond predictive performance, the integration of explainable artificial intelligence through Integrated Gradients provided transparent attribution of model decisions to physiologically meaningful signal segments. The alignment between attribution peaks and tremor-dominant oscillatory regions reinforces the clinical plausibility of the model’s reasoning process. This interpretability component enhances trustworthiness and supports the deployment of the system in clinical decisionsupport environments, where transparency is essential for practitioner acceptance and regulatory compliance. A key innovation of this work lies in the incorporation of a blockchain-based integrity layer within the AI pipeline. The blockchain module ensures immutability, traceability, and tamper-resistant storage of prediction outputs without introducing significant computational overhead. This security
